{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>Israel is My firstborn son.</b> Rabbenu Hananel, of blessed memory, explained: what I have delivered into your hand to enslave them — do not think it is because they are slight in My eyes; I desired only to discipline them, in the manner of what is written (Deuteronomy 8:5): ״וידעת עם לבבך כי כאשר ייסר איש את בנו ה׳ אלהיך מיסרך״ — and you, you have laid your yoke heavily upon them; therefore I am destined to strike your firstborn son — this is the last plague among the ten plagues. And He called them 'My firstborn son Israel' as an expression of distinction and preeminence, in the manner of what is written (Hosea 2:1): ״בני אל חי״, and likewise Mount Sinai, which is holier than all mountains, is called (Isaiah 2:14) ״הר ה׳״, and likewise the Temple, which is holier than any house, is called 'the House of YHWH,' in the manner of what is written (Isaiah 56:7): ״כי ביתי בית תפלה וגו׳״ — end of quotation. And according to this, the sense of this verse and the meaning of the pericope will be: 'See all the signs which I have placed in your hand' — these are the ten plagues — 'and you shall perform them before Pharaoh, and I will harden his heart during the last five plagues, and you shall say to Pharaoh at the last plague, which is the plague of the firstborn: My firstborn son Israel — from now on they shall be redeemed, and the birthright and the preeminence shall be distinguished in them.' And what He says, 'And I say to you: Send forth My son that he may serve Me' — it should have said 'Send forth My firstborn,' since He said 'Israel is My firstborn son.'" "<b>But.</b> The matter is that while they were in exile He called Israel 'My son,' for how could He call them 'My firstborn' — and where is the birthright when they are slaves under their masters' authority and in great bondage? And if you search the Scriptures you will find that just as the Holy One, blessed be He, called Israel 'firstborn' at the plague of the firstborn, so too He called Himself 'firstborn,' as it is written (Exodus 13:2): ״קדש לי כל בכור״ — and the meaning of קדש is 'sanctify Me along with the rest of the firstborn,' and it is like (Exodus 25:2): ״ויקחו לי תרומה״, whose true meaning is 'they shall take Me as a terumah,' and this is why it says ״ויקחו לי תרומה״ — 'the tenth part shall be holy' — and what emerges for us from this is that He called Israel 'firstborn' and Himself, blessed be He, 'firstborn' after the plague of the firstborn, which is the time of redemption — what He did not call them before this — this is explained from the fact that He was with them in the distress of bondage, and the birthright was not distinguished in them, as it were." "<b>And thus.</b> I found in the Book Bahir that the disciples asked Rabbi Eliezer: Rabbi, what is the meaning of what is written ״קדש לי כל בכור״? Was the Holy One, blessed be He, a firstborn? He said to them: ״קדש לי כל בכור״ means nothing other than that He was sanctified and called holy on account of Israel, as it is written ״בני בכורי ישראל״ — He was, as it were, with them at the time of the bondage, and that is why it is written ״שלח את בני ויעבדני״, and He did not say 'Send forth My firstborn.'"], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:56:17.666698973Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}